-e923-52d0-a739-f7b5c411547b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523918 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523918 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523918 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523918 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc7dbb10-4b31-5af6-9ef7-22087cd3d0f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523919 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523919 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523919 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523919 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "feafb06a-b02a-5dd2-94bc-ae63b61d224f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523929 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523929 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523929 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523929 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d3/d330e7c959ede9f969573752b9f67e3ce73c732a680b58cd2f9f466f1cf5db4c.png)\n\nA conical container is $21\\,\\text{ft}$ tall with a diameter of $14\\,\\text{ft}.$ The container is being emptied so that the liquid level lowers at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ At what rate is the liquid volume decreasing when the liquid level is $16\\,\\text{ft}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523929 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c589335-1cfb-54d8-9a5c-d454270a6ef5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523929 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=21\\,\\text{ft}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{14}{2}=7\\,\\text{ft}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the liquid level. We call $h$ the height of the liquid and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the liquid. Both of these quantities, and the liquid volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the liquid must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{7}{21} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (16)^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-56.9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the liquid volume decreases at a rate of approximately $56.9\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523966 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523966 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523966 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523966 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/77/7732722fc875e8cf13482394990baa0b5f8306a8151fca934491bb12dd181d9e.png)\n\nA  $10\\,\\text{cm}$ tall funnel containing water is being drained at a constant rate of  $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The mouth of the funnel is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter. How fast is the water level dropping when there is $15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of water remaining in the funnel?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523966 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "734a1589-9b26-5ca8-b559-8254d0411564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523966 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the funnel. Let's call $H=10\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the funnel, and $R=\\frac{12}{2}=6\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the funnel and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3h}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3h}{5})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of water in the funnel is changing when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pi  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =125 \\\\ h & =5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi ({5}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -10 & =9\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523968 #edb/ref 1071 "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $4$ times its height. Find the rate of the cone's volume increase when height is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "748701f6-8780-55dc-bf9a-808d48cf28f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523968 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $4$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4h \\\\ 2r & =4h \\\\ r & =2h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(2h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's volume is increasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523973 #edb/ref 1071 "The volume of a cone is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always one third of its height.  Find the rate of the cone's height increase when the  height is $6\\,\\text{m}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe height is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7cce7dd9-a52f-5429-96fd-d564ac81f3b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523973 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is one third of the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ 2r & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{6}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{6}h)}^{2}h=\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{36}\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi (6)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's height is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4}\\text{ m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523974 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523974 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523974 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523974 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/86/8676d9a562d254a1194502c5a45368d5345f81aee3a066c28acc87cb1f33812c.png)\n\nThe height of an ice cream cone is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the top of the cone is $2\\,\\text{cm}.$ Ice cream is leaking from a small hole at the bottom at a rate of $2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$ If the ice cream level is $6\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the ice cream level decreasing at that moment?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523974 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d9fecf0-f483-51c1-b6ab-f1ee3ed71254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523974 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cone. Let's call $H=12\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cone, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the ice cream level. We call $h$ the height of the ice cream in the cone and $r$ the radius of the ice cream top surface. Both of these quantities, and the ice cream volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of the ice cream:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cone and the ice cream part of the cone must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{6}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{6})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level in the cone when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi ({6}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\,\\text{cm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the ice cream is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523977 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a9/a9c0d78ce05859db77dcc1e5198596a1a02c798c607ba9c4ff78e6fe1362b113.png)\n\nThe height of a conical cup is $6\\text{ cm}$ and the opening radius is $2\\text{ cm}.$ Water leaks out of the bottom at the rate of $2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ If the water level is $3\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the water level decreasing at that moment?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "829ceea0-b4f0-5bef-b797-d4b161d8e247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523977 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=6\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cup, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{3})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi ({3}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -2 & =\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523989 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523989 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523989 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/80/8036801735554767a33bdee0ff96a95d7e44bc3f97bacb396ec0de6d38f4aafc.png)\n\nA cone is being filled with ice-cream at a rate of $4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The height of the cone is always three times its base radius. At what rate is the radius of the ice cream volume increasing when the radius is $1\\,\\text{cm}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523989 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a637ece-bf3b-5e1e-8874-577affcbf215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523989 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the ice cream volume and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=1\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always three times its base diameter. So, we have $h=3r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =3\\pi (1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4 & =3\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523990 #edb/ref 1071 "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $24\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h}.$ The radius of the cone is always $\\frac{3}{5}$ times its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $6.25\\,\\text{ft}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c01ea5e-36e4-5dc4-b2f4-1bae38b20756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523990 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=24\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6.25\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is $\\frac{3}{5}$ times its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{3}{5}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3}{5}h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi (6.25)^{2}(24) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =337.5\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523993 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e5/e56e31d999674075f3af880e8eadf14cd978f2dba73dd946dc103aae298f6681.png)\n\nAn inverted right circular conical tank contains sand. The height of the tank is $24\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter of its top base is $16\\,\\text{in}.$ The sand is draining from the bottom of the tank so that the height of the sand, $h,$ is decreasing at a constant rate of $6\\,\\text{in/h}.$ Find the rate at which the volume of sand is decreasing when the height of the sand is $9\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nHint: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe sand volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ddf5a2d-9bc3-5a54-8df4-57b5fda24b44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523993 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=24\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical tank, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top base. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the sand level. We call $h$ the height of the sand pile and $r$ the radius of the top surface of the sand. Both of these quantities, and the sand volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the remaining sand must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{24} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of sand is changing when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (9)^{2}(-6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sand volume is decreasing at a rate of $54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523998 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344979" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523998 #edb/ref 1071 "The height of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $4$ times its height. Find the rate of the cone's volume decrease when the height is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer. \n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8aac6cd-427e-5b7e-aea2-44c84e419786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523998 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $4$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4h \\\\ 2r & =4h \\\\ r & =2h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(2h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(-6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524000 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524000 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524000 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524000 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/51/51ee41eea6dfef1292c68a13a438f43c327639e01452e29998bbe513ac3838ba.png)\n\nA container has the shape of a right circular cone. The height of the container is $20\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter at the top of the container is $10\\,\\text{in}.$ Water in the container is evaporating so that its depth, $h$, is decreasing at a constant rate of $\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in/h}.$ At what rate is the volume of water decreasing when the water depth is $12\\,\\text{in}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524000 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9bed4a9-ed2b-5603-85e6-df6b8285a648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524000 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=20\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{10}{2}=5\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{5}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{4})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the container is changing when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi (12)^{2}(-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water is decreasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524006 #edb/ref 1071 "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $14\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$ The radius of the cone is always $7$ times its height. At what rate is the height of the cone decreasing when its height is $2\\,\\text{in}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "babb3260-0ebb-5378-81f6-fb356d66422e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524006 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-14\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=2\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is $7$ times its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (7h)^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{49}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =49\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-14\\pi  & =49\\pi (2)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524011 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a3/a3623402e5d5df9339f1a3bb615c92ff5fcff9ae8d96c3630c5cb847a3038eda.png)\n\nWater is leaking out of a cone-shaped container so that the volume of the water decreases at a rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The height of the container is three times its base radius. Find the rate of decrease of the radius of the water's surface when the radius is $6\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe radius is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc3b1d02-1e54-57cd-be11-d438beee9721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524011 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the conical volume of water and $r$ be the radius of its surface. Both of these quantities, as well as the water's volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we want the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always three times its base radius. So, we have $h=3r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =3\\pi (6)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -12 & =108\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the water's surface decreases at a rate of $\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524015 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f9/f9998b94f05eb6345d375f35f4abc7f88edfc223c4a1af5e7bbc66c4b2a2f0bc.png)\n\nA conical paper cup is $15\\,\\text{m}$ tall with a diameter of $16\\,\\text{m}.$ The cup is being filled with water so that the water level rises at a rate of $1\\,\\text{m/h}.$ At what rate is the water volume increasing when the water level is $10\\,\\text{m}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2f18815-e582-5459-b43c-b02a5b4a0da8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524015 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=15\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the conical cup, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{15} \\\\ r & =\\frac{8}{15}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{8}{15}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi (10)^{2}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈28.4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the water volume increases at a rate of $28.4\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524018 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524018 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524018 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524018 #edb/ref 1071 "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$ The radius of the cone is always equal to its height. At what rate is the height of the cone decreasing when its height is $6\\,\\text{in}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9e85e6a-5a02-52f7-ba3c-489084bc4a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524018 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is equal to its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (h)^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-72 & =\\pi (6)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524020 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cf/cf9fda11d138a5227307070d52fc0a27ce9decc35c91efbb026b89d4507f2ce7.png)\n\nA beverage is pumped into a conical dispenser at a constant rate of  $19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The mouth of the tank is $70\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter and the height of the tank is $40\\,\\text{cm}.$ How fast is the beverage level increasing when the volume of the beverage in the dispenser is $12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e05d5196-22d0-5390-9d3c-413318bc7364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524020 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=40\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the tank, and $R=\\frac{70}{2}=35\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the solution level. We call $h$ the solution level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the solution. Both of these quantities, and the solution volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the solution must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{35}{40} \\\\ r & =\\frac{7h}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{7h}{8})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of the beverage in the dispenser is changing when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.25\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =48 \\\\ h & =2\\sqrt[3]{6}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi (2\\sqrt[3]{6})^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49\\sqrt[3]{36}}{16}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & ≈1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the solution level is increasing at a rate of approximately $1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524023 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/01/01d6575ff056e7f99075597547583b4c648fbe6ad462d38a5d5fd76b8c73a564.png)\n\nA conical container has a height of $8\\,\\text{cm},$ and the radius at the bottom of the container is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ Batter is being poured into the container. If the level of the batter in the container is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}$, find the rate at which the volume of the batter is increasing when the radius of the batter surface is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume of batter is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4ae8f6c-f256-5ee8-80b1-72f1337deae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524023 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=8\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=4\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the batter level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface, $r$ the radius of the cone at the surface of the batter, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the container. All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface decreases as we pour the batter into the container.\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the batter must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{8} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{h}{2} \\\\ h & =3\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}(-\\frac{4}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of batter is increasing at a rate of $9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524037 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524037 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524037 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524037 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/56/561e9e9bd41dddff575a95ed619d2ff3b91a48e0845960a6265c7c7cb1dc04fe.png)\n\nA  $50\\,\\text{cm}$ beverage dispenser is being emptied at a constant rate of  $16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The mouth of the dispenser is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ in radius. How fast is the beverage level dropping when there is $576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of beverage remaining in the funnel?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524037 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f35215fb-23bc-5523-aa38-ea3ed45464a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524037 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the funnel. Let's call $H=50\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the funnel, and $R=50\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the funnel and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{50}{50} \\\\ r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(h)}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of water in the funnel is changing when $v=576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}576\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =1\\,728 \\\\ h & =12\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\pi  & =\\pi (12)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -16 & =144\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the beverage level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
